// batch ai: normandia, montecastelo, ardenas, imphalkohima, taliihantala, iwojima
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "cabeça de praia": "A strip of land taken on the enemy coast by troops who have landed. It is the foothold from which the rest of the army can come ashore and spread out.",
  "desembarque anfíbio": "An attack in which troops leave ships and reach an enemy coast by sea. It is one of the hardest operations in war, because the soldiers arrive exposed, with no cover.",
  "aeroterrestres": "Troops who arrive by air, by parachute or glider, behind enemy lines. In Normandy, they were used to seize bridges and crossroads before the landing.",
  "muralha do atlântico": "A line of fortifications that Germany had built along the coast of occupied Europe, with bunkers, guns, mines and beach obstacles.",
  "mulberry": "A prefabricated artificial harbor. The Allies towed pieces of piers and breakwaters across the English Channel and assembled them on the Normandy coast, because there was no big port to capture.",
  "tanques dd": "Amphibious tanks, with an inflatable canvas skirt that made them float. They could swim from the ship to the beach, but sank easily if the sea was rough.",
  "fortitude": "An Allied deception operation. It made the Germans believe the main attack would come at Calais, with a fictional army, fake radio traffic and decoy targets.",
  "panzer": "A German armored division, made up mainly of tanks. From the German word for armored.",
  "waffen-ss": "The military branch of the SS, the elite organization of the Nazi party. Its divisions fought alongside the regular army and became known for brutality.",
  "ultra": "The name the British gave to decrypted German messages, such as those from the Enigma machine. It gave the Allies clues about the enemy's plans.",
  "linha gótica": "A German defensive line that cut across northern Italy, along the Apennines, from one sea to the other. It was the last great barrier before the Po valley.",
  "pracinhas": "Affectionate nickname for the soldiers of the Brazilian Expeditionary Force. It comes from 'praça', the lowest rank in the Army.",
  "feb": "The Brazilian Expeditionary Force, the corps of about 25,000 men that Brazil sent to fight in Italy, alongside the American Fifth Army.",
  "tropas de montanha": "Soldiers trained to fight on steep, snowy terrain, on skis and with climbing gear. The US 10th Mountain Division was created for this.",
  "lança-chamas": "A weapon that throws a jet of burning fuel. It was used to clear bunkers and caves, where bullets could not reach the defenders.",
  "banzai": "A Japanese war cry. In the Second World War, a 'banzai charge' was a mass attack, on foot and with no hope of coming back.",
  "monção": "The season of very heavy rains in South Asia, from roughly May to September. It turns roads into mud and rivers into torrents.",
  "chindits": "British and Indian deep-penetration troops, commanded by Orde Wingate, who operated behind Japanese lines in Burma.",
  "ponte aérea": "A continuous transport system by aircraft, carrying supplies, soldiers and wounded when the roads are cut.",
  "panzerfaust": "A disposable German anti-tank weapon, light and simple, which the foot soldier fired at close range against armor.",
  "interceptação de rádio": "Listening to and analyzing the enemy's radio messages. Even without breaking the code, it is possible to find out where the units are and when they will attack.",
  "barragem": "Concentrated, continuous artillery fire on an area, to keep the enemy from moving or reacting.",
  "planador": "An aircraft with no engine, towed by another and released near the target. It carried soldiers and weapons silently.",
  "comandos": "Elite troops trained for quick, risky missions, such as storming fortifications or seizing key points.",
  "bunker": "A shelter of concrete or earth, partly buried, from which the defenders shoot and protect themselves from artillery.",
  "porta-aviões de escolta": "A small aircraft carrier, built on a merchant ship hull, used to protect convoys and support landings."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  normandia: {
    war: "Second World War: invasion of German-occupied Europe (1944)",
    hook: "In a single day, more than 6,900 vessels crossed the English Channel to open the door to occupied Europe.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In June 1944, Nazi Germany had occupied western Europe for four years. France had fallen in 1940, and Britain had held out behind the English Channel. In the east, the Red Army was already pushing the Germans back. The Soviets had long been asking the Western Allies to open a second front in Europe, to split Hitler's forces.",
        "But attacking a defended coast is one of the hardest things in war. The [[amphibious landing|desembarque anfíbio]] puts soldiers on the sand exposed, with no cover, facing entrenched enemies. And there was another problem: supply. An army needs thousands of tons a day, and capturing a big, fortified port was almost impossible. The solution was to bring the port along: the [[Mulberry artificial harbors|mulberry]], ready-made pieces that would be towed across the Channel and assembled on the coast.",
        "On the other side, Hitler had ordered the building of the [[Atlantic Wall|muralha do atlântico]]. The project called for 15,000 defended positions held by 300,000 soldiers, but concrete and labor ran short, and most of it was never built. Field Marshal Rommel, put in charge of strengthening the defense, had obstacles placed at the high-tide line and tripled the number of mines.",
        { h: "The road to D-Day" },
        { timeline: [
          ["May 1943", "At the Trident Conference, in Washington, the British and Americans decide to carry out an invasion across the Channel the following year."],
          ["August 1943", "The Quebec Conference accepts the initial plan."],
          ["31 December 1943", "Eisenhower and Montgomery see the plan and expand it: from three to five landing divisions, plus three [[airborne divisions|aeroterrestres]]."],
          ["Early 1944", "A shortage of landing craft pushes the date back, from 1 May to June."],
          ["4 to 6 June 1944", "The attack planned for the 5th is postponed because of the weather. Eisenhower decides to go ahead on the 6th."]
        ]},
        { h: "Where to attack, and how to hide it" },
        "The planners considered four areas: Brittany, the Cotentin peninsula, Normandy and Pas-de-Calais. Calais, where the Channel is narrowest, was the most obvious and the best defended. Normandy was chosen because it had room for the army to grow and was close to the port of Cherbourg.",
        "To keep the Germans looking at Calais, the Allies staged a hoax, [[Operation Fortitude|fortitude]]: an American army that did not exist, the First Army Group, attributed to General Patton. On the very night of the attack, planes dropped strips of aluminum to confuse the radars, fake ships pretended to be a fleet, and dummy paratroopers fell near Le Havre and Isigny."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Dwight Eisenhower, 53 years old" },
        "An American general, he was the supreme commander of the Allied forces. He was not the type who leads from the front: his job was to make Americans, British and Canadians work together and to make the decision no one else wanted to make. The heaviest was about the weather. The attack was set for 5 June, but on 4 June the wind was strong, the sea was rough and the clouds were low. The meteorologist James Stagg predicted a brief improvement for the 6th. The next good dates, with the right tide and moon, would only come on 18 to 20 June, and a storm hit the coast from 19 to 22. Eisenhower ordered the attack to go ahead.",
        { h: "Bernard Montgomery, 56 years old" },
        "He commanded the 21st Army Group, which controlled all the land forces. It was he, together with Eisenhower, who asked to expand the original plan, which called for only three divisions in the first assault.",
        { h: "Erwin Rommel, 52 years old" },
        "The 'Desert Fox' commanded Army Group B, which defended the coast. His idea was that the invader is weakest at the moment he reaches the beach, and so everything should be decided there, with the armor close to the sea. Other commanders, such as Rundstedt, the supreme commander in the West, preferred to keep the reserves farther back and counterattack where the landing was strongest. Hitler chose a middle path: he split up the armor and kept four divisions under his direct control, which only moved on an order from him. On the night of the 5th, Rommel was in Germany. Several accounts say he had traveled to see his wife, who was turning 50, before speaking with Hitler.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Allied side, Omar Bradley commanded the US First Army (Utah and Omaha), Miles Dempsey commanded the British Second Army (Gold, Juno and Sword), and Admiral Bertram Ramsay, the fleet. On the German side, General Friedrich Dollmann commanded the Seventh Army, Leo Geyr von Schweppenburg the Panzer Group West, and Edgar Feuchtinger the 21st [[Panzer|panzer]] Division, the only armored one near the coast."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On the first day about 156,000 Allied soldiers landed: some 73,000 Americans, of whom 15,600 were airborne, and some 83,000 from the British Second Army, among English and Canadians. More than 24,000 men arrived by air, before dawn. The armada brought together almost 7,000 vessels from eight countries: 1,213 warships, 4,126 landing craft, plus auxiliary and merchant ships, with about 195,700 sailors. In the air, the Allies had 9,543 aircraft. The Luftwaffe managed to gather about 815 over Normandy.",
        "The Germans had about 50 divisions in France and the Low Countries, but many were incomplete. On the attacked coast there were around 50,000 men: the 709th Division in the Cotentin (about 12,300, many of them recruits from eastern peoples, the so-called Ostlegionen), the 352nd at Omaha (about 12,000) and the 716th at the British and Canadian beaches (about 7,000, below strength). Near Caen was the 21st Panzer Division, with 146 tanks and 50 assault guns.",
        { note: "Troop numbers vary between sources, depending on whether one counts only those who set foot ashore on the 6th, the airborne, the navy and support. We use those from English Wikipedia, which brings together several sources." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Normandy has wide, sandy beaches, usually with small cliffs or dunes behind. The Allies divided the coast into five sectors, from west to east: Utah and Omaha for the Americans, Gold and Sword for the British and Juno for the Canadians. Omaha was the hardest: high bluffs dominated the sand, and only five ravines led inland, one of them, at Vierville, with a paved road. Behind the beaches lay marshes, flooded fields and the hedgerow country of the interior, where every field becomes a fortress.",
        { h: "The night before" },
        "The Germans had less weather data, because the Allies controlled the Atlantic. The Luftwaffe forecast a storm, and many commanders left their posts. Some were in Rennes, at a war-gaming exercise, and Rommel was far away. On the eve, the coastal radars had been destroyed by air attacks, and the fleet was only spotted at 2 a.m.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the air. Shortly after midnight, the American pathfinders jumped. The clouds got in the way: of the five drop zones, only one was marked accurately. The 101st Airborne Division began jumping around 1:30 a.m., to control the passes behind Utah; many men landed far from the target, and some drowned in flooded fields. The 82nd arrived around 2:30 a.m.: on the east bank of the Merderet River, 75% landed near the right spot and took Sainte-Mère-Église within two hours, but on the west bank only 4% landed in the planned area. The British had better luck: at 12:16 a.m., a [[glider|planador]] assault took the bridges over the Caen canal and the Orne River, the famous Pegasus Bridge, intact and with few casualties. The Merville battery, however, which threatened the British landing, was attacked by only 160 of the 600 men who were supposed to go. Lieutenant Colonel Terence Otway attacked anyway and destroyed the guns, with 75 casualties. They were 75 mm guns, not 150 mm, as had been feared.",
        "Second, the German reaction was slow. The Seventh Army learned of the jumps at 1:20 a.m., but Rundstedt did not at first believe it was a large invasion. The 21st Panzer was set in motion at 2 a.m., but Feuchtinger needed authorization from the supreme command and only received orders near 9 a.m. On his own initiative, he formed a battle group.",
        "Third, the sea. At 5:45 a.m. the naval bombardment began, and at 6:30 a.m. the first soldiers set foot on the sand. The current and the wind pushed the landing craft to the east. At Utah, the 4th Division landed about 1,800 meters from the planned spot, and some of the day's objectives were left for later, partly because the landing went too far south. About 21,000 men came ashore, with only 197 casualties, and the strong points were neutralized around noon.",
        "Fourth, Omaha. It was the disaster. The 1st and 29th Divisions met not one German regiment, as expected, but the entire 352nd Division. The bombers released their loads too late, and almost all the obstacles were left intact. Of the 32 amphibious tanks (the [[DD tanks|tanques dd]]) launched some 4.6 km from the beach, 27 sank, with 33 crewmen dead. At 8:30 a.m. the landings were suspended to clear the beach. By the end of the morning, only some 600 men had reached the top. General Norman Cota, assistant commander of the 29th, was seen everywhere, pushing men off the sand, and won the Distinguished Service Cross. It took the whole day, and late afternoon, to establish a narrow [[beachhead|cabeça de praia]]. Casualties for the day at Omaha are estimated at 2,000 to 2,400.",
        "Nearby, 200 men of the 2nd Ranger Battalion, under Lieutenant Colonel James Rudder, climbed the 30-meter cliffs of Pointe du Hoc to destroy a battery of guns. When they reached the top, the guns had already been moved. The Rangers found them in an orchard, some 550 meters to the south, and destroyed them. At dawn on the 7th, Rudder had only 90 men fit to fight. Relief arrived on the 8th.",
        "Fifth, the eastern beaches. At Gold, the tide delayed the landing to 7:25 a.m. The guns at Longues-sur-Mer were silenced by cruisers, and the fort at Le Hamel only at 4 p.m., by a demolition charge from a special tank. At Juno, the rough sea delayed the Canadians, who landed ahead of their tanks and suffered 961 casualties. At Sword, 21 of the 25 amphibious tanks reached land, and the French [[commandos|comandos]] of Philippe Kieffer cleared the Riva Bella casino.",
        "Sixth, the counterattack. At 4 p.m., the 21st Panzer attacked between Sword and Juno and almost reached the Channel. It stopped in front of the British 3rd Division and was called back. According to accounts, it was only around 4 p.m. that Hitler released the armored divisions that Rundstedt had been asking for since morning."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "By the end of the 6th, the Allies had five beachheads, but none of the day's objectives had been fully reached. Carentan, Saint-Lô and Bayeux were still German, and Caen, which was supposed to fall on the first day, was only taken in July. Only Gold and Juno were linked. The five beaches only joined up on 12 June, along a front of about 97 km by 24 deep.",
        "Allied losses on the first day were estimated at no fewer than 10,000, with 4,414 confirmed dead: 2,499 Americans and 1,915 from other countries. German losses were between 4,000 and 9,000 dead, wounded, missing or captured. About 3,000 French civilians died on D-Day and the day after.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The invasion opened the second front. Within a few weeks, more than a million Allied soldiers had landed, 39 divisions in all. Paris was liberated in August, and Germany surrendered in May 1945, 11 months later. D-Day became the symbol of the liberation of western Europe and a model of joint operations among the armies, navies and air forces of several countries.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The hoax had an effect: a deception operation convinced Hitler that the main attack would be at Calais, and the German reserves were slow to reach Normandy." },
        { note: "A much-told story says Hitler was asleep and no one dared wake him to release the armor. Accounts vary: some say the order went out around 4 p.m., others that it was later. What is certain is that the German chain of command, too centralized, lost precious hours." },
        { note: "The air and naval bombardment was supposed to have destroyed the defenses before the landing, but at Omaha the low clouds and the bombers' haste made most of the bombs fall behind the German positions. Many historians see in this one of the causes of the casualties." },
        { note: "On the eve, Eisenhower wrote a note taking all the blame in case the attack failed. He never needed to use it." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  ardenas: {
    war: "Second World War: Western Front, winter of 1944-1945",
    hook: "Hitler's last gamble: on 16 December 1944, 400,000 Germans came out of the fog to try to reach Antwerp.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the autumn of 1944, Germany seemed lost. Since D-Day, the Allies had liberated Paris and advanced to the German border. In the east, the Red Army was advancing. Many Allies expected the war to end by Christmas. But Hitler still had an idea.",
        "On 16 September 1944, he presented the plan to his generals: a quick strike through the Ardennes, the region of forests and hills in Belgium and Luxembourg, through which the Germans had already passed in 1940. The target was Antwerp, the great port the Allies depended on. If the Germans got there, they would split the Allied armies, encircle four of them and, Hitler hoped, force the Westerners to negotiate peace. With that, he could send everything east. The generals doubted it, and the plan was given the code name Wacht am Rhein, 'Watch on the Rhine', to sound defensive.",
        "The Americans saw the Ardennes as a quiet sector. It was where they put new troops, still without experience, and units tired from other fronts to rest. The lines were thin.",
        { h: "The road to the Ardennes" },
        { timeline: [
          ["August 1944", "Romania, Germany's source of oil, falls to the Soviets; fuel runs short."],
          ["16 September 1944", "Hitler presents the plan for the offensive through the Ardennes."],
          ["28 November 1944", "The port of Antwerp begins operating, after being cleared of mines in the Scheldt estuary."],
          ["16 December 1944", "The German attack begins, early in the morning, with a bombardment by 1,600 guns along 130 km."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Adolf Hitler" },
        "He made the decision almost alone, against the opinion of many generals. He hoped bad weather would cancel the Allies' air advantage and that the shock of a big offensive would shake the unity between Americans and British. The strategic calculation, however, was unrealistic: Germany had neither the fuel nor the reserves to sustain a long offensive.",
        { h: "Gerd von Rundstedt and Walter Model" },
        "Rundstedt was the supreme commander in the West, and Model, the commander of Army Group B, which carried out the attack. Both thought the objective of Antwerp too ambitious, and proposed smaller goals, but Hitler did not accept. The Sixth Panzer Army, under Sepp Dietrich, would attack in the north, the Fifth, under Hasso von Manteuffel, in the center, and the Seventh, under Erich Brandenberger, in the south.",
        { h: "Eisenhower, Bradley, Patton and Montgomery" },
        "On the Allied side, Eisenhower was the supreme commander. Omar Bradley commanded the 12th Army Group, to which the US First and Third Armies belonged. George Patton commanded the Third, and Bernard Montgomery, the British 21st Army Group. In the crisis, Eisenhower made a controversial decision: on 20 December, he passed the US First and Ninth Armies, north of the gap, to Montgomery's command, since Bradley's communications had been cut.",
        { h: "Anthony McAuliffe, 46 years old" },
        "The brigadier general who commanded the 101st Airborne Division at Bastogne, in place of the division commander, Maxwell Taylor, who was in the United States. He was an artilleryman, not a career paratrooper, and went down in history for a single word."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Germans gathered more than 410,000 men (406,342, according to a count of 16 December), a little over 1,400 tanks and armored vehicles, about 2,600 guns and more than 1,000 aircraft. There were 13 infantry divisions, 2 paratrooper divisions and 6 armored divisions taken from the high command's reserve. The Americans had few soldiers in the region at the start. Over the course of the battle, the Allies grew to more than 700,000 men, and in the first week alone 250,000 were moved to the area.",
        { note: "Troop strength figures depend on the date and the method. The 600,000 Americans and 400,000 Germans on the battle card are the most cited; Wikipedia gives more than 700,000 Allies in total, and more than 410,000 Germans." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Dense forest, hills and narrow roads, with snow and ice in winter. For armor, which depends on roads, the Ardennes are a bottleneck: one crossroads and one bridge are enough to stop an entire column. Two towns were vital, because they are road junctions: Saint-Vith, in the north, and Bastogne, in the south. Whoever held the crossroads controlled the pace.",
        { h: "The secret and the weather" },
        "The Germans achieved complete surprise. The Allies were overconfident, misread German intentions, flew little because of the bad weather and were looking at their own offensives in other sectors. [[Ultra|ultra]] managed to decrypt 40 to 50 messages a day and saw the Luftwaffe quadruple its fighters and use the word 'Jägeraufmarsch' ('fighter concentration'), but the surprise was complete anyway. Heavy clouds covered the sky and kept Allied aviation from flying.",
        { h: "The battle in four movements" },
        "First, the shock. On 16 December, early in the morning, the bombardment opened the offensive on a 130 km front. The German infantry broke through the thin lines, and the armor ran through the gaps. The following afternoon, on 17 December, near the Baugnez crossroads, close to Malmedy, troops of the [[Waffen-SS|waffen-ss]] from Joachim Peiper's battle group captured American soldiers of the 285th Field Artillery Observation Battalion and opened fire on the prisoners: at least 84 were killed. The Malmedy massacre made the Americans fight with rage, and Peiper and Dietrich were tried for it after the war. Peiper's group had about 4,800 men and 600 vehicles, but not enough fuel to reach Antwerp. It was delayed by fallen bridges and American resistance, and ended up stopped at La Gleize. On 23 December, Peiper abandoned the vehicles and escaped on foot, with some 800 men.",
        "Second, the defenses on the flanks. In the north, the 2nd and 99th Infantry Divisions held the Elsenborn ridge against the Sixth Panzer Army, blocking the most important roads. It is considered the decisive component of the battle. In the center, the 7th Armored Division, under Robert Hasbrouck, with the 106th and other units, held out at Saint-Vith and delayed the Fifth Panzer Army: the Germans had expected to take the town at 6 p.m. on the 17th, and Montgomery only ordered it evacuated on 21 December. There was also Operation Greif, in which English-speaking German soldiers, in American uniforms, infiltrated behind the lines, under the command of Otto Skorzeny, to seize the Meuse bridges. They took none, but they created confusion and made the Allies stop to check papers at roadblocks.",
        "Third, Bastogne. The town, a junction of seven roads, was defended by the 101st Airborne Division, by a wing of the 10th Armored Division and by the 969th Artillery Battalion, made up of Black soldiers. On 21 December it was surrounded. Medicine was lacking, and artillery ammunition was rationed to ten rounds per gun per day. On 22 December, the German commander, General von Lüttwitz, sent a demand for surrender. On receiving the note, McAuliffe said to his staff: 'Ah, nuts!' ('What nonsense!'). The written reply that went to the Germans was a single word: 'Nuts!'. On 24 December, the sky cleared, and Allied aviation returned to attack troops and supply lines, and to drop supplies by parachute.",
        "Fourth, the turnaround. On 19 December, the Allied commanders met in a bunker in Verdun. Eisenhower said the situation was 'one of opportunity, not disaster'. Patton said he would attack with two divisions within 48 hours, and had already had his army's 90-degree turn to the north prepared. On 26 December, the 4th Armored Division broke the encirclement: Company D of the 37th Tank Battalion reached Bastogne at 4:50 p.m. The westernmost point the Germans reached was Foy-Notre-Dame, near Dinant, where the US 2nd Armored Division stopped them on 24 December. On 27 December, the offensive was broken. The fighting continued for almost a month, until the line was restored to where it had been before. On 1 January, the Luftwaffe tried a last gamble, Operation Bodenplatte: it attacked Allied airfields in the Low Countries and destroyed or damaged some 465 aircraft, but lost 277, and never recovered."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The offensive failed. The Germans ran out of fuel: they had lost the Romanian oil fields in August 1944. The Sixth Panzer Army was stopped in the north, and the westernmost point the Germans reached was Foy-Notre-Dame, near the Meuse River. The battle ended, by the most widely used date, on 25 January 1945.",
        "German losses are between 63,000 and 104,000, among dead, wounded, missing and prisoners, plus about 527 to 554 tanks and assault guns and some 800 aircraft. American ones vary by source: somewhere between 75,000 and 89,000 casualties, among dead, wounded and missing. About 3,000 civilians died.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the largest and bloodiest battle in the history of the United States Army in the Second World War, and the last great German offensive in the west. It exhausted what remained of German armor and aviation, which were never replaced, and opened the way for the invasion of Germany. The Allies crossed the Rhine in March and met the Soviets in April.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The name: 'Battle of the Bulge' was invented by the press, because of the bulge the German offensive drew on newspaper maps. The official Allied name was the Ardennes Counteroffensive; for the Germans, Operation Wacht am Rhein." },
        { note: "The number of American dead is debated. English Wikipedia gives 8,407 dead, 46,170 wounded and 20,905 missing. Other sources, such as the US Army and the Battle Monuments Commission, speak of about 19,000 dead and more than 23,000 prisoners. The difference lies in how the missing are classified and which reports are added together." },
        { note: "Skorzeny's hoax: soldiers captured in American uniforms were shot as spies. Skorzeny himself was tried at Dachau, in 1947, and acquitted." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  montecastelo: {
    war: "Second World War: Italian campaign (1943-1945)",
    hook: "The pracinhas came from a tropical country to an icy mountain, and it took them three months to take it.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Brazil entered the Second World War in August 1942, after German and Italian submarines sank Brazilian ships. But for a long time it seemed the war would be only at sea. From that came an irony: people said it would be easier for a snake to smoke than for Brazil to send soldiers to Europe. The soldiers of the [[FEB|feb]] adopted the phrase as a war cry and put a snake smoking a pipe on their emblem.",
        "The Brazilian division, of about 25,000 men, left Rio de Janeiro in July 1944 and was incorporated into the US Fifth Army, in Italy. Its first combat was in September, in the Serchio River valley, where it took Massarosa, Camaiore and Monte Prano. In November, the division was transferred to the Reno River valley, in the Apennines. Its forward headquarters was in Porretta Terme, under General Mascarenhas de Moraes.",
        "There, the Germans held the [[Gothic Line|linha gótica]], a chain of defenses along the mountains. The American general Mark Clark, commander of the 15th Army Group, wanted to open the way to Bologna before the heaviest snows. The US IV Corps, to which the Brazilian division was attached, had to take the mountain passes. The obstacle was Monte Castelo, at 977 meters, which dominated State Road 64, the Porrettana, the only all-weather road between Pistoia and Bologna. From there, German artillery saw and fired on everything that moved.",
        { h: "The road to Monte Castelo" },
        { timeline: [
          ["August 1942", "Brazil declares war on the Axis."],
          ["July 1944", "The first FEB soldiers leave for Italy."],
          ["September 1944", "First FEB combat, in the Serchio valley."],
          ["24 November 1944", "First attack on Monte Castelo, together with American troops. The Germans counterattack and retake the points they had lost."],
          ["29 November 1944", "Second attack, by the Brazilian division alone. It fails."],
          ["12 December 1944", "Third attack, with two battalions of the 1st Regiment. It fails, with about 150 casualties in a single day."],
          ["21 February 1945", "The last attack, within Operation Encore. The FEB takes the summit."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "João Baptista Mascarenhas de Moraes" },
        "The general who commanded the Brazilian division and the whole FEB. After the failure of 12 December, he concluded that the summit could only be taken by an attack of the whole division, and not by isolated battalions. He waited for the right moment, with enough artillery and the help of the Americans.",
        { h: "Eccard von Gablenz" },
        "The German general commanded the 232nd Infantry Division, which defended the region. He was a veteran of Stalingrad. The division had been formed on 22 June 1944, with about 9,000 men, a mix of recruits and veterans of the eastern front, many recovering from wounds. It had three regiments, the 1043rd, the 1044th and the 1045th. In the final months it received reinforcements, such as the 4th Mountain Battalion.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Majors Franklin, Uzeda and Sizeno Sarmento commanded the three battalions of the final attack. General Oswaldo Cordeiro de Farias commanded the division's artillery. On the American side, the IV Corps was commanded by General Willis Crittenberger, and the 10th Mountain Division attacked the neighboring mountain."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On the Allied side, the Brazilian division, of about 25,000 men, and, in the final attack, the US 10th Mountain Division, made up of [[mountain troops|tropas de montanha]]. On the German side, the 232nd Division, with about 9,000 men, on high ground in well-prepared positions, with [[bunkers|bunker]] and artillery that covered every path.",
        { h: "The terrain and the climate" },
        "The Apennines are high, with slopes that rise steeply, and the mountain road is at the mercy of whoever controls the top. That winter, rain and air bombardments turned the paths to mud, and the tanks could not move. Then came the cold: about 20 degrees below zero, not counting the wind, according to Portuguese Wikipedia. The Brazilians, from a tropical country, were not used to it.",
        { h: "The first attacks" },
        "On 24 November, the Brazilian reconnaissance squadron and the 3rd Battalion of the 6th Infantry Regiment joined the American Task Force 45 and took Monte Belvedere, but the Germans counterattacked and drove them out of Monte Castelo. On 29 November, three Brazilian battalions, with three American tank platoons, attacked at 7 a.m. Rain and low cloud prevented air support, and the mud bogged down the tanks. The Germans repelled the attack, and in the afternoon the Brazilians were back at the starting point, with about 190 casualties. The night before, the Germans had already retaken Belvedere, exposing the Allied left flank.",
        "On 12 December, the 2nd and 3rd Battalions of the 1st Regiment attacked in bad weather. German artillery stopped them: about 150 casualties, 20 of them dead. It was one of the FEB's days of heaviest losses. Mascarenhas concluded that the mountain would only fall to an attack by the whole division.",
        { h: "The final attack" },
        { formation: true },
        "On 18 February 1945, the Fifth Army's Operation Encore began, whose objectives included Monte Castelo, Belvedere and Della Torraccia. The 10th Mountain Division, to the left of the Brazilians, had been attacking Belvedere since the 18th and was to take Della Torraccia at 5 p.m. on the 21st. On the night of the 20th, the Brazilian division had put its three infantry regiments in position.",
        "At 6 a.m. on the 21st, the main attack began. The 1st Battalion, under Major Franklin, climbed head-on. The 2nd, under Major Uzeda, supported from the right, and the 3rd, under Major Sizeno Sarmento, went in from advanced positions, occupied during the night. Between 4 and 5 p.m., the artillery under Cordeiro de Farias laid down a concentrated [[barrage|barragem]] on the summit, which the Wikipedia article recognizes as responsible for a good part of the success. Around 5:30 p.m., Franklin's men reached the top, before the Americans took Della Torraccia. The Brazilians took over the captured bunkers and fortifications. The Americans only finished clearing Della Torraccia after dark."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The FEB took Monte Castelo on 21 February 1945. Casualty figures vary: English Wikipedia, citing the historian Donato, gives 417 Brazilian dead and wounded and 8 prisoners, against about 950 dead and wounded of the American 10th Division, and at least 23 German prisoners. The German total of dead and wounded is uncertain.",
        "After Monte Castelo, the FEB continued through the mountains. In April 1945 it fought at Montese, its bloodiest battle, and on 29 April it surrounded and forced the surrender of the German 148th Division and other units, at Fornovo di Taro: there alone were 14,779 prisoners, and more than 20,000 in all.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The soldiers of the FEB, the [[pracinhas|pracinhas]], were the only Latin Americans to fight on land in Europe. The FEB lost 454 Army dead, plus five pilots of the fighter group, and came home in the second half of 1945. The FEB's return helped precipitate the fall of Getúlio Vargas and the end of the Estado Novo, according to Portuguese Wikipedia.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Some count four failed attacks before the victory, and some count three (24 November, 29 November and 12 December). The Brazilian Army also cites a thrust on the 25th. The difference lies in how each day's operations are separated." },
        { note: "The origin of the smoking snake: the expression was born as irony from those who doubted Brazil would enter the war. The pracinhas adopted it, and the FEB emblem shows a snake with a pipe." },
        { note: "The mountain, taken on 21 February, is in Gaggio Montano, in the Emilia-Romagna region. There is a monument nearby, in Abetaia, and a memorial in the Brazilian cemetery in Pistoia." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  imphalkohima: {
    war: "Second World War: Burma campaign (1944)",
    hook: "The Japanese army carried food for three weeks and tried to invade India in the middle of the monsoon.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1944, Japan had dominated Burma for two years, after driving out the British in 1942. On the other side of the border lay British India. The region is one of jungles, steep ranges and wide rivers, with few roads, and the rains of the [[monsoon|monção]], from May on, stop everything.",
        "Lieutenant General Renya Mutaguchi, commander of the Japanese Fifteenth Army, wanted more than to defend the border. The plan he proposed, Operation U-Go, began as a preventive attack against the British IV Corps at Imphal and became an invasion of India itself. Mutaguchi may have been encouraged by the first expedition of the [[Chindits|chindits]], in 1943, whose losses the Allies hid. Imperial headquarters approved the plan on 7 January 1944.",
        "Imphal was the Allies' great supply base, linked by road, through the Naga Hills, to the railhead at Dimapur. If Mutaguchi isolated Imphal and took Dimapur, the British position in eastern India would collapse. He expected to win in three weeks.",
        { h: "The road to Imphal" },
        { timeline: [
          ["February 1944", "In the Arakan, the Japanese surround a British base, the 'Admin Box'. The garrison is supplied by air and holds out, and the invaders run out of food."],
          ["5 March 1944", "The air phase of the second Chindit expedition begins, behind Japanese lines."],
          ["8 March 1944", "The battle of Imphal begins."],
          ["About 15 March 1944", "The Japanese 31st Division crosses the Chindwin River, heading for Kohima, with three weeks of supplies."],
          ["4 April 1944", "The Japanese reach Kohima."]
        ]},
        "Mutaguchi's division commanders had doubts, above all about supply. The general suggested the solution that became famous, the 'Genghis Khan' rations: herds of oxen and buffalo that would cross the river with the troops and be slaughtered along the way. Most died for lack of pasture, and the meat rotted before it arrived. The planners also assumed that tanks could not operate in the hills and left most of the artillery behind."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "William Slim, 52 years old" },
        "He commanded the British Fourteenth Army, made up largely of Indian soldiers. He was a low-key general, without the swagger of others, and had learned from the defeats of 1942. In 1944, he used transport aircraft to supply and move his divisions while the Japanese, without supplies, wore themselves out. He was knighted at the end of the year, at Imphal.",
        { h: "Geoffry Scoones" },
        "He commanded IV Corps, which defended Imphal. He had to sustain a siege of months with scarce reserves.",
        { h: "Renya Mutaguchi" },
        "Ambitious, he believed the war could be decided in India. On 6 June, he and his superior, Kawabe, already privately accepted that success was impossible, but neither ordered a withdrawal. Mutaguchi only ended the offensive on 3 July. Both were later removed.",
        { h: "Kotoku Sato" },
        "The commander of the Japanese 31st Division, which attacked Kohima, had disliked Mutaguchi since factional disputes in the 1930s and doubted the plan from the start. On 25 May he warned that he would withdraw on 1 June if he was not supplied, and on 31 May he left Kohima even against orders. In the Japanese army, that was unheard of. After the battle, he was relieved; he refused to commit seppuku and demanded a court-martial, but the high command declared him mentally unfit to be tried.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the British side, Colonel Hugh Richards commanded the Kohima garrison and General Montagu Stopford, the XXXIII Corps. The Indian nationalist leader Subhas Chandra Bose had an army allied with Japan, the Indian National Army (INA), which fought alongside the Japanese."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On the Allied side, about 120,000 men in total, most of them from British India (the majority were Indian). On the Japanese side, about 85,000. The 31st Division that attacked Kohima numbered 15,000 to 20,000. The Japanese used one tank regiment, the 14th, with 66 light tanks, and left most of the artillery behind.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Jungle-covered ranges, with wide rivers and few roads. Imphal sits in a plain surrounded by hills, where the airfields were. The road to Dimapur passes through Kohima, a ridge that dominates the road. It was possible to fight from hill to hill, and the war was decided by possession of the ridges and by supply.",
        { h: "The battle in four movements" },
        "First, the surprise that was not. In the south, the Indian 17th Division withdrew from Tiddim along a long, exposed road, pressed by the Japanese 33rd Division, and reached the Imphal plain on 4 April. Farther north, the 50th Parachute Brigade was badly mauled at Sangshak, and the Japanese 15th Division cut the northern road and threatened Imphal. In March, Mountbatten took 20 C-46 aircraft off the airlift over the Himalayas, the 'Hump', and Slim used the aircraft to carry the Indian 5th Division from the Arakan, with its artillery and vehicles, in 11 days. It was the beginning of an [[airlift|ponte aérea]] that decided the battle.",
        "Second, Kohima. The garrison was some 2,500 men, a thousand of them noncombatants, plus the 1st Battalion of the Assam Regiment, the 4th Battalion of the Royal West Kent Regiment and other units. On 6 April the siege began. The center of the fight was the bungalow of the district commissioner, the DC, and the tennis court in front of it: the two sides were so close that they threw grenades from one trench to the other. The garrison was squeezed into an ever smaller perimeter, on Garrison Hill. On 17 and 18 April, the Japanese took the Kuki Picquet post and cut the garrison in two. On 18 April, the Indian 161st Brigade fought its way through with the support of aircraft, artillery and tanks, and on 20 April the British 6th Brigade relieved the original garrison.",
        "Third, the stalemate and the rains. At Bishenpur, to the south, the Japanese 33rd Division was stopped some 16 km from Imphal by British artillery and a lack of supplies. At Shenam, to the east, the fight for the peaks lasted from 8 to 22 April. On 13 April, the 5th Division retook the Nungshigum ridge, with light tanks, on terrain the Japanese had judged too steep for armor. With the [[monsoon|monção]] in May, mud hampered both sides, but the Allies were supplied by air.",
        "Fourth, the collapse. At Kohima, the British took the positions on 11 May, and on 13 May a tank climbed onto the tennis court, and the Dorsetshire Regiment cleared the ridge. Hunger was decisive: the rice depot the Japanese had taken at Naga was destroyed by bombing. The 23rd Long Range Penetration Brigade cut the supply lines. On 31 May, Sato abandoned Naga and the other positions, and the XXXIII Corps advanced south. On 22 June, the troops from Kohima and from Imphal met at the 109-mile marker, some 16 km from Imphal, and the siege was over. On 3 July, Mutaguchi ended the offensive, and the Japanese withdrew to the Chindwin, leaving behind artillery, transport and wounded."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Japanese losses at Imphal were 54,879 dead and wounded, of whom 13,376 dead, according to Wikipedia, most of them victims of hunger, disease and exhaustion. The Allies lost 12,603 dead and wounded at Imphal and 4,064 at Kohima. At Kohima, the Japanese had at least 5,764 combat casualties, with many other deaths from disease, hunger or suicide. The Japanese lost almost all of their 12,000 pack animals and 30,000 head of cattle.",
        "Air supply was decisive. By the end of the battle, the Allies had flown in 19,000 tons of supplies and 12,000 men, and evacuated 13,000 wounded and 43,000 civilians and noncombatants.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Operation U-Go cost the Japanese Fifteenth Army almost all of its pack animals and a good part of its strength, and was followed by the Allied reconquest of Burma in 1945. It was a victory of logistics and organization: the Allies, supplied by air, held positions that the Japanese, without food, could not take. It is worth remembering that the soldiers on the British side were, for the most part, Indian.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Wikipedia calls the two battles, together, the 'Stalingrad of the East'. It is a comparison of effect, not of size." },
        { note: "The Kohima epitaph, in the cemetery where the tennis court stood, is famous: 'When you go home, tell them of us and say: for your tomorrow, we gave our today.' It is attributed to John Maxwell Edmonds, and recalls the epitaph of the Spartans at Thermopylae. The cemetery holds 1,420 Allied dead." },
        { note: "Three soldiers won the Victoria Cross at Kohima, the highest British decoration: John Harman, John Randle and Jemadar Abdul Hafiz, 18 years old, the youngest soldier of the British Indian army to receive it." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  taliihantala: {
    war: "Continuation War, Finland against the Soviet Union (1941-1944)",
    hook: "A country of 4 million people stopped the largest Soviet offensive against Finland, and remained independent.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Finland fought the Soviet Union twice in the Second World War. The first, the Winter War of 1939-1940, ended with the loss of territory. The second, the Continuation War, began in 1941, when the Finns attacked alongside the Germans to recover what they had lost. In 1944, the Germans were in retreat everywhere. In January, the siege of Leningrad had been broken.",
        "Stalin then decided to knock Finland out of the war. The Soviet offensive on the Karelian Isthmus began on 9 June 1944, three days after D-Day, in coordination with the invasion of Normandy. The Finns fell back through two lines of defense, and Viipuri (Vyborg), the country's second-largest city, fell on 20 June. On 21 June, the Soviet high command ordered the Leningrad Front to reach Lake Saimaa.",
        { h: "The road to Tali-Ihantala" },
        { timeline: [
          ["9 June 1944", "The Soviet offensive begins on the Karelian Isthmus."],
          ["12 June 1944", "Marshal Mannerheim asks Germany for help."],
          ["16 June 1944", "The German Kuhlmey air detachment arrives."],
          ["20 June 1944", "Viipuri falls."],
          ["22 June 1944", "Ribbentrop demands that Finland keep fighting. President Ryti personally gives this guarantee."],
          ["25 June 1944", "The battle of Tali-Ihantala begins."]
        ]},
        "In exchange for help, the Germans set a price. They sent aircraft, an assault-gun brigade, a division and, above all, [[panzerfausts|panzerfaust]]. In return, President Risto Ryti signed a personal commitment not to make a separate peace with Moscow, the so-called Ryti-Ribbentrop agreement. Attempts to negotiate peace failed: the Soviet reply of 23 June demanded a declaration of surrender, and Finland refused it."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Carl Gustaf Mannerheim, 77 years old" },
        "The Finnish commander-in-chief, a marshal since 1933, a former officer of the tsar's army. He asked Germany for help on 12 June, and what came was little: mostly weapons and some air support.",
        { h: "Karl Lennart Oesch" },
        "He commanded the Isthmus Forces, the headquarters that directed the defense. He worked with Lieutenant General Taavetti Laatikainen's IV Corps and with the Armored Division, under Major General Ruben Lagus, the only armored division of the Finnish army.",
        { h: "Leonid Govorov" },
        "A Soviet marshal, he commanded the Leningrad Front. The 21st Army, under Dmitri Gusev, led the attack on Tali-Ihantala, and the 23rd, under Aleksandr Cherepanov, attacked toward the Vuoksi River.",
        { h: "Vilho Nenonen" },
        "The Finnish artillery general. The fire-correction method that bears his name made it possible to concentrate the fire of many batteries on a single target."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers are disputed. For the Finns, English Wikipedia speaks of about 50,000 men, with later estimates near 100,000. For the Soviets, Soviet sources give 48,000 to 60,000, while Western estimates reach 150,000. The German side sent the Kuhlmey air detachment (Fw 190, Ju 87 Stuka and Bf 109) and the 303rd Assault Gun Brigade, with 22 StuG III and 9 StuH 42.",
        { note: "We use 'about 50,000 against 150,000' because it is the most cited count, but it is not secure: the real ratio between the two sides is disputed, and some estimates give the Finns as many as 100,000 men." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An area of about 100 km², 8 to 14 km northeast of Viipuri, between the northern tip of Viipuri Bay and the Vuoksi River. It is terrain of lakes and forests, which limits where tanks can go.",
        { h: "The battle in four movements" },
        "First, the attack at Tali. On 25 June, after a heavy bombardment by artillery and aircraft, the Soviets attacked at 7:30 a.m., from Tali, and the 30th Guards Rifle Corps joined the fight. The Finns found themselves at risk of encirclement, but a counterattack with the reserve and the Armored Division pushed the Soviets back, west of Lake Leitimojärvi, and almost annihilated the Soviet 27th Tank Regiment.",
        "Second, the fighting of 27 to 30 June. The Finns reorganized into two battle groups and tried to encircle Soviet divisions at Talinmylly. They came to within about a kilometer of closing the ring, but did not manage it. On 28 June, a [[barrage|barragem]] by 14 artillery battalions destroyed or damaged at least 15 tanks at Ihantala. The 29th was the worst day for the Finns, and they left Tali on 30 June.",
        "Third, Ihantala. On 1 and 2 July came the hardest fighting, with Finnish losses of about 800 men a day. The Finnish artillery made the difference: by Nenonen's method, 21 batteries, some 250 guns, could fire on a single target. In total, they fired more than 122,000 shells. On 2 July, Finnish [[radio interception|interceptação de rádio]] picked up a message announcing a Soviet attack for 3 July, at 4 a.m. Before it began, 40 Finnish and 40 German bombers attacked, and 250 guns fired about 4,000 shells on the troops still assembling. Finnish and German anti-tank weapons, among them the panzerfaust and the Panzerschreck, destroyed many armored vehicles, 25 of them in a single afternoon.",
        "Fourth, the end. The Soviets had some success on 6 July and were driven back the next day. Afterward, they shifted their effort to the Vuoksi and sent troops to the Narva front. From 9 July they stopped trying to break through, and on 10 July the Soviet high command ordered a shift to the defensive."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Finland lost about 8,750 men: 1,350 dead, 1,100 missing and 6,300 wounded, by English Wikipedia's count. The Finns estimate that the Soviets lost about 600 tanks, most of them to air attack, artillery and close combat, and 284 to 320 aircraft. The historian Ohto Manninen estimates 18,000 to 22,000 dead and wounded in the 21st Army, but with uncertainty. The Germans lost 33 aircraft.",
        "On 28 July, Ryti resigned and asked Mannerheim to accept the candidacy for the presidency. This freed Finland from the personal commitment Ryti had signed with Ribbentrop. The ceasefire began at 7 a.m. on 4 September 1944, although the Red Army only stopped 24 hours later.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the largest battle in the history of the Nordic countries. Several historians, such as Jowett, Snodgrass, McTeer, Lunde, Alanen and Moisala, argue that it and other defensive victories convinced the Soviet leadership that conquering Finland would be too costly. Finland lost territory, but remained independent and, unlike the countries of Eastern Europe, did not become a Soviet republic.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The artillery victories have numbers to be read with care: Wikipedia points out that the Finns fired more shells at Vuosalmi, in eight days, and on the U line, in five days. The record for fire, therefore, is not Tali-Ihantala's." },
        { note: "Interviews with Soviet prisoners, cited by Finnish researchers, indicate that the intention was to advance to Helsinki. The interception of Soviet radio communications is pointed to by Lunde as one of the factors in the defense." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  iwojima: {
    war: "Second World War: Pacific War (1945)",
    hook: "The 21 km² island where the Americans lost more men than the Japanese.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "At the start of 1945, the United States had already taken the Mariana Islands, from which B-29 bombers attacked Japanese cities. But a stopover was missing. Between the Marianas and Tokyo lay the volcanic island of Iwo Jima, part of the Volcano Islands, with two airfields, the South Field and the Central Field. From the Japanese point of view, it was an early-warning station: it warned by radio of the bombers' arrival. From the American point of view, it was a possible base for P-51 Mustang fighters, which would escort the B-29s, and a place for damaged bombers to land in an emergency.",
        "With the loss of the Marianas, in 1944, the Volcano Islands became even more important to Japan.",
        { h: "The road to Iwo Jima" },
        { timeline: [
          ["June 1944", "General Kuribayashi is appointed to command the defense. Air and naval bombardments begin the same month."],
          ["From June 1944", "The Japanese dig tunnels and interconnected positions."],
          ["16 to 18 February 1945", "Three days of naval and air bombardment before the landing."],
          ["19 February 1945", "The Marines land."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Tadamichi Kuribayashi, 53 years old" },
        "The Japanese lieutenant general who commanded the defense. He had served in the United States and Canada and knew American industrial power. He knew the island could not be defended to victory, and hoped the number of casualties would make the Americans think twice before invading Japan itself. He rejected defense at the beach and chose defense in depth, with positions that supported one another, linked by tunnels. He forbade mass [[banzai|banzai]] charges, although his superiors ordered, against his will, some defense at the beach.",
        { h: "Holland Smith, Harry Schmidt and Raymond Spruance" },
        "Admiral Spruance commanded the fleet. General Holland Smith commanded the expedition's land forces, and Major General Harry Schmidt, the V Amphibious Corps, which carried out the landing. Schmidt asked for ten days of naval bombardment, but Rear Admiral Blandy, commander of fire support, refused, because he needed time to resupply ammunition, and granted three.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "The Marine divisions were the 5th (Rockey), the 4th (Cates) and the 3rd (Erskine, floating reserve). On the Japanese side, Baron Takeichi Nishi, 1932 Olympic equestrian champion, commanded the tanks, which were camouflaged and used as fixed artillery."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Japan had about 21,000 men (21,060 according to Wikipedia), with about 60% of normal ammunition and food for four months at most. The United States landed about 30,000 Marines on the first day and 40,000 more afterward, some 70,000 in total. Wikipedia lists 110,000 men of all branches, counting sailors and support.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A volcanic island of 21 km², with Mount Suribachi at the southern tip, and in the north the Motoyama plateau, of rocks, caves and ravines. The sand is black, volcanic ash: it bogs down vehicles and does not let you dig a hole for cover. Kuribayashi had dug 18 km of tunnels, of the 27 planned. The tunnel that was to link Suribachi to the rest of the island was left incomplete, and the mountain area ended up working as an almost independent sector.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the landing. On 19 February, the first wave arrived at 8:59 a.m., one minute ahead of schedule, on a clear day. The Japanese held their fire for about an hour, while the beach filled with men, and opened fire shortly after 10 a.m. Behind the beach, the slope of some 5 meters, of loose ash, was almost impossible to climb. The tracks of the amphibious vehicles spun, and the Seabees had to open roads with tractors. The Marines discovered that bunkers cleared with [[flamethrowers|lança-chamas]] were often reoccupied through the tunnels, and suffered many casualties. On the right end, the 3rd Battalion of the 25th Regiment landed about 900 men; by nightfall, only 150 were fit to fight.",
        "Second, Suribachi. The 28th Regiment, under Colonel Harry Liversedge, crossed the island at its narrowest point, some 800 meters, and isolated the mountain. On 21 February, the only airborne kamikaze attack of the battle sank the [[escort carrier|porta-aviões de escolta]] Bismarck Sea and badly damaged the Saratoga. On 23 February, a patrol under Lieutenant Harold Schrier reached the top and raised a small flag, attached to a Japanese pipe. It was the first foreign flag to fly on Japanese soil. The Secretary of the Navy, James Forrestal, wanted the flag as a keepsake, and so a larger one was sent, which six Marines raised. It was this second raising that the photographer Joe Rosenthal, of the Associated Press, recorded, and the photo won the Pulitzer the same year. Three of the six men, Michael Strank, Harlon Block and Franklin Sousley, died days later, and later investigations corrected the identity of some of those pictured.",
        "Third, the north. In the north, the rocky terrain favored the defense. The Motoyama plateau, with Hill 382 and the 'Turkey Knob', and a depression between them called 'the Amphitheater', formed what the Marines called the 'meat grinder'. The Japanese hid during the bombardments and came out afterward to shoot at those advancing. General Erskine ordered a night attack by the 9th Regiment, without prior bombardment, which took Hill 362. On 8 March, the 3rd Division reached the north coast, splitting the Japanese defenses in two. On the night of the 8th, Captain Samaji Inouye led about 1,000 men in a [[banzai|banzai]] charge, and the Marines counted 784 dead Japanese the next morning.",
        "Fourth, the 'secure' island. At 6 p.m. on 16 March, 25 days after the landing, the island was declared secure. But the 5th Division was still facing Kuribayashi's last stronghold, in a ravine in the northwest. On 21 March, the Marines destroyed the command post with four tons of explosives, and on 24 March they sealed the remaining caves. On the night of 25 March, about 300 Japanese attacked near Airfield 2, with heavy losses, and killed 53 Americans and wounded 120. Kuribayashi may have led this attack personally, but that is disputed. The island was declared secure, for good, at 9 a.m. on 26 March."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The United States had 6,821 dead and 19,217 wounded in the main phase, plus 2,648 cases of combat fatigue, and 15 dead and 144 wounded afterward. On the Japanese side, between 17,845 and 18,375 dead or missing, and only 216 prisoners in the main phase. After the battle, the 147th Infantry Regiment spent three months hunting hidden soldiers, with flamethrowers, grenades and explosive charges: it killed about 1,602 and captured 867.",
        "According to Wikipedia, it is a unique case among the amphibious battles of the Pacific: total American casualties were greater than the Japanese, something like three Americans for every two Japanese.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The flag on Suribachi became a symbol, and Admiral Nimitz wrote that, among the Americans who served on Iwo Jima, 'uncommon valor was a common virtue'. In all, 27 men received the Medal of Honor for the battle: 22 Marines and five sailors. The lessons of Iwo Jima were used to plan Okinawa and the invasion of Japan, which ended up not taking place.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Was it worth it? The former Chief of Naval Operations William Pratt argued that the island was useless to the Army as a base and to the Navy as a fleet base. Holland Smith complained, after the war, that the lack of naval fire support cost the lives of Marines throughout the island campaign. Defenders of the attack recall that the island served as an emergency landing field for bombers." },
        { note: "According to Schmidt himself, the Marines had only about 13 hours of naval fire support in the 34 hours of daylight available before the landing." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

